          "site_admin": false
        },
        "body": "# Basic functionality\r\n\r\nYes! The last contribution of 2024! A new wakeword runtime, which is the fastest implementation available for openwakeword models. Completely self-trainable, using the [jupyter notebook](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1q1oe2zOyZp7UsB3jJiQ1IFn8z5YfjwEb?usp=sharing)  (in future will be merged in this library). \r\n\r\n## Architecture\r\n\r\nThe implementation still uses the original openwakeword architecture using these stages: \r\n1. Melspectrogram; A pre-processing model that computes a melspectrogram of the input audio data. This library uses an ONNX implementation of Torch's melspectrogram function with fixed parameters which enables efficient performance across devices. This onnx model is provided by [dscripka](https://github.com/dscripka/openWakeWord).\r\n\r\n2. Embedding/feature generation; A shared feature extraction backbone model that converts melspectrogram inputs into general-purpose speech audio embeddings. This model is provided by Google as a TFHub module under an Apache-2.0 license. For openWakeWord, this model was manually re-implemented to separate out different functionality and allow for more control of architecture modifications compared to a TFHub module. The model itself is series of relatively simple convolutional blocks, and gains its strong performance from extensive pre-training on large amounts of data. This model is the core component of openWakeWord, and enables the strong performance that is seen even when training on fully-synthetic data.\r\n\r\n3. Feature Classification: A classification model that follows the shared (and frozen) feature extraction model. The structure of this classification model is arbitrary, but in practice a simple fully-connected network or 2 layer RNN works well. \r\n\r\n## Design\r\n\r\nThe design was made to be as simple as possible, to make it more maintainable. So that means the implementation is single threaded and runs all the models sequentially. Instead of trying to sync multiple threads. Which seems to be less performing anyway. As the processor is continually waiting for synchronization between threads.\r\n\r\n## How to use\r\nThere is a guide included for how to use on various platforms. It should support Windows out of the box. But Linux and Mac is preferred. When trying on Windows, you don't need to actually install anything if you have ever tried to compile face detector. As it uses the same toolchain and has all dependencies included in CMake. \r\n\r\nLinux and Mac need to install libsdl2-dev or SDL2 package (see guide for details)\r\n\r\n### Performance\r\n\r\nI did some profiling, but very basic. So take it with a grain of salt.\r\n\r\n| ms per ww. (lowwi) | ms per ww. (old implementation) |\r\n|--|--|\r\n| 0.12 | 20 |\r\n\r\nThe average cpu load with lowwi is around ~2.5%, while the old implementation was around 15%.\r\nThe cpu load doesn't scale linearly though. As with around <10 models the cpu load with lowwi starts to increase. Stating that the load isn't caused by the ww classifiers but mostly by audio processing/copying.\r\n\r\nThere is still alot of improvement possible. Many devices now come with NPU or optimized cpu runtimes for ONNX. As this uses ONNX. It could easily be adopted to use hw optimized algorithms. Which speeds up things even more. I am also sure that these models are light enough to run on a google coral edge tpu. But that only works with litert (there are also litert (new name for tensorflow lite) models available).\r\n\r\n## Docs\r\n\r\nI added just like the other projects some documentation on how to setup project on various os-es. And some basic api explanation stuff.\r\n\r\n## Example\r\n\r\nThere are two examples; One which uses your microphone to listen you scream \"Hey mycroft\". And the fragment examples which loads in an .wav with some synthetic voice data (which speaks out \"Hey mycroft\") and tests it against an empty audio buffer. Just two quick tests. A thorough benchmark and unit-test still have to be made.\r\n\r\n\r\n## Fun fact! It works even when whispering!\r\nI was shocked on how well it recognized me whispering \"hey mycroft\" at the middle of the night, while my parents were trying to sleep on the floor beneath me ;)\r\n\r\n## Workflow\r\n\r\nLast but not least, some workflows for automatically building linux & windows when doing new pr. So we can test for compilation without having to run on our own pc. And workflow that generates mkdocs automatically. \r\n\r\nEnjoy! \r\n\r\nVictor",
